#!/usr/bin/env etcs

# ONE NODE, BOTH SEATS LOCAL. This is the whole boot: a ChessNode is the
# ordering domain a ChessGame needs (ChessGame::streamOf() answers null, and
# every verb refuses, with none in front of it -- see ChessNode.h), so the
# node exists here for that reason alone. Nothing about rendering lives in
# this script because nothing about the board is drawn by ETCS: the page is
# the same DOM grid of squares the server-authoritative chess.html already
# used, just fed from this runtime instead of from fetch(). See this folder's
# README, "Why no canvas", for the rest of that reasoning -- and for why
# there is no `detach` here, no window, no frame pump: a ChessGame is text
# state, not pixels, so nothing here needs one. The runtime's own REPL
# worker (ETCS_REPL_SHELL, on for every web loader build) is what keeps this
# session alive regardless, exactly as it does for ShellProvider's own
# terminal-only page.

spawn ChessProvider::ChessNode game
game.Mount(game)

# Warms the match into existence so the page's FIRST call is a move, not a
# race with lazy match creation (ChessNode::createGameLocked runs on the
# first Request that names a match). Spent here instead of on the first
# click, which is also what tells the terminal the board is actually up
# before anyone has touched it.
game.Request(game/white/local/fen)

# THE SAME NODE THE HOSTED ONE IS. What follows is chess_web.etcs
# (NetworkProvider/scripts) minus its static pages: the same HttpServer,
# the same route (every /game/... path answered by this node's Request, the
# listing verbs rooms and players included), the same CORS header. Every
# line of it takes in a browser except the last: Start() asks the OS for a
# listening socket and a browser has none to give, so ConnectionManager
# logs `listen() failed`, HttpServer logs `not started`, and the page goes
# on -- which is the honest outcome, and the point. This node is a name
# server in kind; what it lacks is an address anyone else can dial, and
# that is the peer link's job (README, "Name servers, all the way down").
ensure NetworkProvider::HttpServer web
web.SetPort(8080)
web.AddRoute(@game Request @game Filter)
web.AddHeader(Access-Control-Allow-Origin *)
web.Start()
